  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-linear-multistep-method-consistency-order-and-root-condition": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $k\\ge1$, $d\\ge1$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be the right-hand side of a first-order initial-value problem on an open set $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$, using the convention of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. A **constant-step $k$-step linear multistep method** has real coefficients $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_k,\\beta_0,\\ldots,\\beta_k$ with $\\alpha_k\\ne0$ and advances values by\n\n$$\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_jY_{n+j}=h\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_j f(t_{n+j},Y_{n+j}),\\qquad t_{n+j}=t_n+jh,\\quad h>0.$$\n\nMultiplying all coefficients by the same nonzero scalar leaves the method unchanged; one may normalize $\\alpha_k=1$. Its **characteristic polynomials** are\n\n$$\\rho(\\zeta)=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_j\\zeta^j,\\qquad \\sigma(\\zeta)=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_j\\zeta^j.$$\n\nFor an exact solution $y$ with $p+1$ continuous derivatives, the **unscaled local residual** at $t_n$ is\n\n$$\\tau_n(h;y)=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_jy(t_n+jh)-h\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_jy'(t_n+jh).$$\n\nThe method has **order at least $p$**, where $p\\ge1$ is an integer, if this residual is $O(h^{p+1})$ uniformly for admitted starting times in every fixed compact interval on which the indicated derivatives are bounded. It is **consistent** if it has order at least one. Equivalently, with the above coefficient convention, consistency is $\\rho(1)=0$ and $\\rho'(1)=\\sigma(1)$.\n\nThe **root condition** is that every complex root of $\\rho$ has modulus at most one and every root of modulus one has algebraic multiplicity one. The method is **zero-stable** if for each $T>0$ there is a constant $M_T$ independent of $N$ such that every solution of the homogeneous recurrence $\\sum_j\\alpha_jY_{n+j}=0$ on the uniform grid $h=T/N$ satisfies\n\n$$\\max_{0\\le n\\le N}\\|Y_n\\|_\\infty\\le M_T\\max_{0\\le j<k}\\|Y_j\\|_\\infty.$$\n\nHere $\\|x\\|_\\infty=\\max_{1\\le i\\le d}|x_i|$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. The root-condition equivalence for zero-stability is established separately."
    ],
    "lem-variable-step-adams-lagrange-weights": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k\\ge1$ and let $t_{n+1}>t_n>\\cdots>t_{n-k+1}$ be distinct time nodes. For $j=0,\\ldots,k-1$, let\n\n$$L_j^{P}(s)=\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le\\ell<k\\\\\\ell\\ne j}}\\frac{s-t_{n-\\ell}}{t_{n-j}-t_{n-\\ell}},\\qquad b_j^n=\\int_{t_n}^{t_{n+1}}L_j^P(s)\\,ds.$$\n\nThese coefficients give the explicit Adams predictor\n\n$$Y_{n+1}^P=Y_n+\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}b_j^nf(t_{n-j},Y_{n-j})$$\n\nobtained by integrating the degree-$(k-1)$ interpolant through the known derivative values $f(t_{n-j},Y_{n-j})$.\n\nFor the corrector, let $\\widehat L_j(s)$, $j=0,\\ldots,k$, be the Lagrange basis on the $k+1$ distinct nodes $t_{n+1},t_n,\\ldots,t_{n+1-k}$ and define $\\widehat b_j^n=\\int_{t_n}^{t_{n+1}}\\widehat L_j(s)\\,ds$. These are the coefficients of the integrated degree-$k$ interpolant, with $j=0$ the endpoint derivative at $t_{n+1}$. The fully implicit corrector is\n\n$$Y_{n+1}=Y_n+\\sum_{j=0}^{k}\\widehat b_j^nf(t_{n+1-j},Y_{n+1-j}),$$\n\nwhere the $j=0$ term uses the unknown new endpoint value."
    ],
    "alg-adams-pece-one-correction": [
      "## Definition\n\nInput an integer $k\\ge1$, a strictly increasing finite grid $t_0<\\cdots<t_N$ with $N\\ge k$, starting states $Y_0,\\ldots,Y_{k-1}\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and a vector field $f$ defined at every requested evaluation. On a dimension mismatch or a grid with repeated or decreasing nodes, return `invalid-input` or `invalid-grid`. Evaluate and store $F_j=f(t_j,Y_j)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,k-1$; an undefined evaluation returns `evaluation-failure` with the valid prefix.\n\nFor $n=k-1,\\ldots,N-1$, construct the predictor weights $b_j^n$ and corrector weights $\\widehat b_j^n$ from the actual nodes as in [[lem-variable-step-adams-lagrange-weights]], then do the following:\n\n1. Predict $Y_{n+1}^P=Y_n+\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}b_j^nF_{n-j}$.\n2. Evaluate $F_{n+1}^P=f(t_{n+1},Y_{n+1}^P)$. If undefined, return `evaluation-failure` with phase `predictor`.\n3. Correct once by setting $Y_{n+1}=Y_n+\\widehat b_0^nF_{n+1}^P+\\sum_{j=1}^{k}\\widehat b_j^nF_{n+1-j}$.\n4. Evaluate and store $F_{n+1}=f(t_{n+1},Y_{n+1})$ for the next predictor. If undefined, return `evaluation-failure` with phase `post-corrector` and the computed $Y_{n+1}$.\n\nAfter all steps return `completed` with the states. This is one predictor and one corrector pass; it does not solve the fully implicit Adams corrector equation and asserts no convergence, stability, or error bound."
    ],
    "lem-companion-powers-bounded-under-root-condition": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $C$ be the row-shift companion matrix for the monic polynomial $\\rho/\\alpha_k$, where $\\rho$ is the characteristic polynomial of a $k$-step method. For a complex vector $v$, set $\\|v\\|_\\infty=\\max_i|v_i|$ and for a matrix $A$ set $\\|A\\|_\\infty=\\max_i\\sum_j|A_{ij}|$. Then\n\n$$\\sup_{n\\ge0}\\|C^n\\|_\\infty<\\infty$$\n\nif and only if $\\rho$ satisfies the root condition from [[def-linear-multistep-method-consistency-order-and-root-condition]]. Consequently the homogeneous recurrence is zero-stable exactly under that root condition."
    ],
    "lem-linear-multistep-order-conditions-from-taylor": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor the method in [[def-linear-multistep-method-consistency-order-and-root-condition]], define\n\n$$\\mu_0=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_j,\\qquad \\mu_m=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_jj^m-m\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_jj^{m-1}\\quad(m\\ge1).$$\n\nFor every integer $p\\ge1$, the method has order at least $p$ if and only if $\\mu_m=0$ for each $m=0,\\ldots,p$. In particular, consistency is equivalent to $\\rho(1)=0$ and $\\rho'(1)=\\sigma(1)$."
    ],
    "lem-variable-step-bdf2-interpolation-and-local-defect": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $h_{n-1},h_n>0$, $r_n=h_n/h_{n-1}$, and let $y$ be an exact solution with three continuous derivatives near $t_{n-1},t_n,t_{n+1}$, where $h_j=t_{j+1}-t_j$. The derivative at $t_{n+1}$ of the quadratic interpolant through $(t_{n+1},y_{n+1})$, $(t_n,y_n)$, and $(t_{n-1},y_{n-1})$ is\n\n$$\\frac{(1+2r_n)y_{n+1}-(1+r_n)^2y_n+r_n^2y_{n-1}}{h_n(1+r_n)}.$$\n\nEquating this derivative to $f(t_{n+1},Y_{n+1})$ defines the variable-step BDF2 formula\n\n$$\\frac{(1+2r_n)Y_{n+1}-(1+r_n)^2Y_n+r_n^2Y_{n-1}}{h_n(1+r_n)}=f(t_{n+1},Y_{n+1}).$$\n\nFor an exact vector solution $y'=f(t,y)$ whose third derivative is bounded, the unscaled residual\n\n$$\\tau_n=(1+2r_n)y(t_{n+1})-(1+r_n)^2y(t_n)+r_n^2y(t_{n-1})-h_n(1+r_n)y'(t_{n+1})$$\n\nhas norm $\\|\\tau_n\\|_\\infty=O(h_n^3)$ uniformly whenever $r_n$ stays in a fixed compact subinterval of $(0,\\infty)$; the formula and remainder hold componentwise."
    ],
    "alg-variable-step-bdf2-with-selected-implicit-branch": [
      "## Definition\n\nInput a vector field $f(t,y)$, a finite strictly increasing grid $t_0<\\cdots<t_N$, starting states $Y_0,Y_1\\in\\mathbb R^d$ (so $N\\ge1$), and for each later step a prescribed closed ball $B_{n+1}\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ that specifies the admissible implicit branch. Set $h_n=t_{n+1}-t_n$ and $r_n=h_n/h_{n-1}$ for $1\\le n<N$. If a time increment is nonpositive or the starting dimensions do not agree, return `invalid-grid` or `invalid-input`.\n\nFor $n=1,\\ldots,N-1$, form the equation\n\n$$\\frac{(1+2r_n)Z-(1+r_n)^2Y_n+r_n^2Y_{n-1}}{h_n(1+r_n)}=f(t_{n+1},Z).$$\n\nIf this equation has exactly one root $Z$ in $B_{n+1}$, set $Y_{n+1}=Z$ and continue. If it has no root there, return `no-root` with the computed prefix; if it has more than one root there, return `ambiguous-branch` with the computed prefix. If an evaluation of $f$ is undefined, return `evaluation-failure` with the prefix. On reaching $N$, return `completed` and $(Y_0,\\ldots,Y_N)$. The procedure is defined for arbitrary positive adjacent step ratios; no convergence or accuracy property is part of this algorithm.\n\nThe coefficients are those obtained from the quadratic interpolation derivative in [[lem-variable-step-bdf2-interpolation-and-local-defect]]. A separate theorem gives the uniform ratio hypotheses needed for second-order convergence; they are not part of this algorithm."
    ],
    "thm-dahlquist-equivalence-for-linear-multistep-methods": [
      "## Statement\n\nFix a constant-step $k$-step method from [[def-linear-multistep-method-consistency-order-and-root-condition]] on $[0,T]$. Convergence means that for every admitted first-order IVP and every sequence of starting values whose errors at $t_0,\\ldots,t_{k-1}$ tend to zero as $h=T/N\\to0$, the maximum grid error tends to zero. For exact solutions with two continuous derivatives, choose a compact state set $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with the exact trajectory in its interior and suppose $f$ is Lipschitz in the state on $[0,T]\\times K$. For an implicit method, require a unique selected root in $K$ for every history in $K$. The method converges for every such IVP if and only if it is consistent and satisfies the root condition. More precisely, if its order is at least $p\\ge1$, the exact solution has $p+1$ continuous derivatives, and the starting errors are $O(h^p)$, then its global error is $O(h^p)$.\n\nFor the sufficiency and order estimates, the branch assumption means that a unique selected root exists in $K$ for every history in $K$; the error proof then keeps the computed histories there. Convergence is tested on defined runs. In the necessity tests $y'=0$ and $y'=1$, the implicit equation has a unique root in all of $\\mathbb R^d$ at every step because $\\alpha_k\\ne0$ and the right-hand side is independent of the new state, so those tests remain defined even if an unstable mode leaves the sufficiency tube. The global estimate does not assert existence of an implicit root for arbitrary IVPs or step sizes."
    ],
    "lem-variable-step-bdf2-increment-ratio-recurrence": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor the homogeneous vector-valued variable-step BDF2 recurrence obtained from [[alg-variable-step-bdf2-with-selected-implicit-branch]] by setting $f=0$, let $\\Delta_n=Y_n-Y_{n-1}\\in\\mathbb R^d$. For every $r_n=h_n/h_{n-1}>0$,\n\n$$\\Delta_{n+1}=q(r_n)\\Delta_n,\\qquad q(r)=\\frac{r^2}{1+2r}.$$\n\nThe multiplier satisfies $q(r)\\le1$ exactly when $0<r\\le1+\\sqrt2$. In particular, if $0<r_n\\le r_*<1+\\sqrt2$ for every step, then $q(r_n)\\le q_*:=r_*^2/(1+2r_*)<1$ uniformly."
    ],
    "thm-variable-step-bdf2-order-two-under-ratio-bound": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $y'=f(t,y)$ on $[0,T]$, with $y:[0,T]\\to\\mathbb R^d$ having three continuous derivatives. Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be compact with $y([0,T])\\subset\\operatorname{int}K$, and suppose $f$ is defined on $[0,T]\\times K$ and Lipschitz in its state variable there with constant $L$ in the max norm. Let $h_n=t_{n+1}-t_n>0$, $t_0=0$, $t_N=T$, $H=\\max_n h_n\\to0$, and suppose\n\n$$0<r_{\\min}\\le r_n:=\\frac{h_n}{h_{n-1}}\\le r_*<1+\\sqrt2\\qquad(1\\le n<N).$$\n\nAssume $\\|Y_0-y(t_0)\\|_\\infty+\\|Y_1-y(t_1)\\|_\\infty=O(H^2)$. For each step prescribe a closed branch ball $B_{n+1}\\subset K$ as in [[alg-variable-step-bdf2-with-selected-implicit-branch]], and suppose that for every pair of preceding states in $K$ the step equation has exactly one root in $B_{n+1}$. Select that root. Then for sufficiently small $H$ all computed states remain in $K$ and\n\n$$\\max_{0\\le n\\le N}\\|Y_n-y(t_n)\\|_\\infty=O(H^2).$$\n\nThe lower ratio bound controls the local residual constant; the upper bound controls the parasitic increment. This is a separate variable-grid theorem and does not follow from the constant-step root condition alone."
    ],
